before:
```typescript
type Paint = string | [number, number, number];
const broad: Record<string, Paint> = {sky: "blue"};
// broad.sky could be text or a tuple, so narrow it before string-only use.
const precise = {sky: "blue"} satisfies Record<string, Paint>;
console.log(precise.sky.toUpperCase());
```

after:
Paint allows text or a three-number tuple. This check still lets TypeScript know that sky is text.
```typescript
type Paint = string | [number, number, number];
const precise = { sky: "blue" } satisfies Record<string, Paint>;
console.log(precise.sky.toUpperCase());
```
The output is BLUE. ToUpperCase returns uppercase text without changing the original string.
